From: Kreijstal <elektrischrainbow@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 05:09:25 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 09/10] pbd: initialize RWLock's shared_mutex eagerly on MinGW

PBD::RWLock wraps std::shared_mutex. With mingw-w64's libstdc++ that is
a pthread_rwlock_t statically initialized with
PTHREAD_RWLOCK_INITIALIZER, which winpthreads turns into a real lock
lazily on the first lock call. That lazy step (rwlock_static_init) is
not thread-safe: if two threads take a fresh lock concurrently, one of
them gets EINVAL back, std::shared_mutex only checks for EDEADLK, and
the thread proceeds as if it held the lock. The result is unsynchronized
access to whatever the lock protects (RCU managers, route/processor
lists, ...), i.e. rare, hard to reproduce corruption.

Take and release the write lock once in the RWLock constructor, while
the object is still private to the constructing thread, so every RWLock
is fully initialized before it can be shared.
---
 libs/pbd/rwlock.cc | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/libs/pbd/rwlock.cc b/libs/pbd/rwlock.cc
index 2cf14aa..7bcb1c4 100644
--- a/libs/pbd/rwlock.cc
+++ b/libs/pbd/rwlock.cc
@@ -22,6 +22,19 @@ using namespace PBD;
 
 RWLock::RWLock ()
 {
+#ifdef __MINGW32__
+	/* libstdc++ implements std::shared_mutex with a pthread_rwlock_t set to
+	 * PTHREAD_RWLOCK_INITIALIZER. winpthreads initializes such locks lazily
+	 * on first use, and that lazy initialization is not thread-safe: when
+	 * two threads hit a fresh lock at the same time, the loser gets EINVAL,
+	 * which libstdc++ ignores, and continues without holding the lock.
+	 * Take and release the lock once while the object is still private to
+	 * the constructing thread so that it is fully initialized before it can
+	 * be shared.
+	 */
+	_rw_lock.lock ();
+	_rw_lock.unlock ();
+#endif
 }
 
 RWLock::~RWLock ()
